G-Retriever formulates graph-based retrieval as a Prize-Collecting Steiner Tree (PCST) optimization problem to **retrieve a connected, semantically relevant subgraph**—not just isolated top-k nodes or edges—while **balancing relevance (prizes) against size/complexity (edge costs)**. This ensures the retrieved subgraph:  
- Preserves structural context and neighborhood relationships critical for multi-hop reasoning;  
- Includes “bridge” nodes/edges that connect relevant components but may have lower individual similarity scores;  
- Remains manageable in size (e.g., eliminating 99% of nodes in the WebQSP dataset), which substantially reduces computational complexity and speeds up running time (e.g., from 18.7 min/epoch to 6.2 min/epoch);  
- Enables explainability by returning a coherent, connected subgraph grounded in the original graph structure.  

As stated in the paper: *“Our approach assigns higher prize values to nodes and edges more relevant to the query… while minimizing the total costs of its edges”*, and *“it retrieves connected subgraphs, capturing not just nodes or edges with high relevance scores, but also those that act as ‘bridges’ connecting other highly relevant nodes and edges.”*

